                "content": "**Notation used**\n\n*\u202fRh is a Group\u20119 metal (neutral\u202fRh = d\u2078\u202fs\u00b9).  \n*\u202fX\u2011type ligand = anionic two\u2011electron donor (charge \u20131).  \n*\u202fL\u2011type ligand = neutral two\u2011electron donor.  \n\nThe **ionic (oxidative\u2011state) method** proceeds as follows  \n\n1. Assign the overall charge of the complex.  \n2. Add the formal charges of the ligands; the remainder is the metal oxidation state.  \n3. From the neutral\u2011atom configuration of Rh (d\u2078\u202fs\u00b9) subtract the number of electrons removed to obtain the \\(d^{n}\\) count.  \n4. Total valence\u2011electron count = \\(d^{n}\\) electrons + 2\u202fe\u207b supplied by each X\u2011type ligand + 2\u202fe\u207b supplied by each L\u2011type ligand.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (1)   Oxidation state, \\(d^{n}\\) configuration and electron count\n\n| Complex | Overall charge | Ligand charges (X\u2011type\u202f\u20131, L\u2011type\u202f0) | Rh oxidation state | \\(d^{n}\\) | Ligand donation (e\u207b) | Total e\u207b |\n|---------|----------------|--------------------------------------|--------------------|----------|----------------------|----------|\n| **A** = \\([\\text{Rh I}_{2}(\\text{CO})_{2}]^{-}\\) | \u20131 | 2\u202f\u00d7\u202f(\u20131) = \u20132 | \\((-1)-(-2)=+1\\) \u2192 **Rh(I)** | neutral Rh (d\u2078\u202fs\u00b9) \u2192 remove 1\u202fe\u207b \u2192 **d\u2078** | 2\u202fI\u207b\u00b72\u202fe\u207b + 2\u202fCO\u00b72\u202fe\u207b = 8\u202fe\u207b | **16\u202fe\u207b** |\n| **B** = \\([\\text{Rh I}_{3}(\\text{CH}_{3})(\\text{CO})_{2}]^{-}\\) | \u20131 | 3\u202f\u00d7\u202f(\u20131) + (\u20131) = \u20134 | \\((-1)-(-4)=+3\\) \u2192 **Rh(III)** | remove 3\u202fe\u207b \u2192 **d\u2076** | 3\u202fI\u207b\u00b72\u202fe\u207b + CH\u2083\u207b\u00b72\u202fe\u207b + 2\u202fCO\u00b72\u202fe\u207b = 18\u202fe\u207b | **18\u202fe\u207b** |\n| **C** = \\([\\text{Rh I}_{3}(\\text{C(O)CH}_{3})(\\text{CO})]^{-}\\) | \u20131 | 3\u202f\u00d7\u202f(\u20131) + (\u20131) = \u20134 | **Rh(III)** | **d\u2076** | 3\u202fI\u207b\u00b72\u202fe\u207b + acyl\u207b\u00b72\u202fe\u207b + CO\u00b72\u202fe\u207b = 16\u202fe\u207b | **16\u202fe\u207b** |\n| **D** = \\([\\text{Rh I}_{3}(\\text{C(O)CH}_{3})(\\text{CO})_{2}]^{-}\\) | \u20131 | 3\u202f\u00d7\u202f(\u20131) + (\u20131) = \u20134 | **Rh(III)** | **d\u2076** | 3\u202fI\u207b\u00b72\u202fe\u207b + acyl\u207b\u00b72\u202fe\u207b + 2\u202fCO\u00b72\u202fe\u207b = 18\u202fe\u207b | **18\u202fe\u207b** |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (2)   Classification of the elementary steps\n\n| Step | Transformation | Change in Rh oxidation state? | Change in total e\u207b count? |\n|------|----------------|------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| **A \u2192 B** | Oxidative addition of CH\u2083I (adds CH\u2083\u207b and I\u207b) | **+2** (I \u2192 III) | **+2\u202fe\u207b** (16\u202f\u2192\u202f18) |\n| **B \u2192 C** | Migratory insertion of coordinated CO into Rh\u2013CH\u2083 bond | **No change** (III \u2192 III) | **\u20132\u202fe\u207b** (18\u202f\u2192\u202f16) |\n| **C \u2192 D** | Association of an external CO ligand (ligand addition) | **No change** (III \u2192 III) | **+2\u202fe\u207b** (16\u202f\u2192\u202f18) |\n| **D \u2192 A** | Reductive elimination of acetyl iodide (CH\u2083COI) | **\u20132** (III \u2192 I) | **\u20132\u202fe\u207b** (18\u202f\u2192\u202f16) |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (3)   Net catalytic reaction and the dual role of CO\n\nAdding the four elementary steps cancels the intermediate species, leaving only the reagents and products that appear once:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\text{CH}_{3}\\text{I} + \\text{CO} \\;\\longrightarrow\\; \\text{CH}_{3}\\text{COI}\n}\n\\]\n\nThe catalyst \\([\\text{Rh I}_{2}(\\text{CO})_{2}]^{-}\\) is regenerated and therefore does not appear in the net equation.\n\n**Why CO is both a ligand and a stoichiometric reactant**\n\n* One CO molecule **coordinates** to the 16\u2011e\u207b Rh centre (step\u202fC\u2192D) and remains bound in the regenerated catalyst A; this CO is \u201crecycled\u201d and does not appear in the overall stoichiometry.\n* The second CO molecule **undergoes migratory insertion** (step\u202fB\u2192C); after insertion it becomes part of the acyl fragment that is expelled as the carbonyl carbon of acetyl iodide. Hence that CO is consumed stoichiometrically.\n\nConsequently the net reaction consumes one CO molecule even though the catalyst always carries two CO ligands.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (4)   Isotopic\u2011labelling experiment  \n\n**Initial situation** \u2013 Catalyst A possesses two CO ligands, one \\(^ {13}\\text{CO}\\) and one \\(^ {12}\\text{CO}\\).  \n\n**Assumptions**  \n\n1. In B the two CO ligands are chemically equivalent.  \n2. During migratory insertion either CO may be selected with probability \u00bd.  \n3. No CO exchange occurs before insertion.  \n4. The externally added CO in step\u202fC\u2192D is pure \\(^ {12}\\text{CO}\\).\n\n#### Atom\u2011tracking\n\n| Pathway (probability) | Which CO inserts? | Carbonyl carbon of product CH\u2083COI | CO ligands on regenerated A |\n|-----------------------|-------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------|\n| **Path\u202f1** (\u00bd)        | \\(^ {13}\\text{CO}\\) inserts \u2192 acyl carbon = \\(^ {13}\\text{C}\\) | **\\(^ {13}\\)C** in acetyl iodide | The remaining original CO is \\(^ {12}\\text{CO}\\); together with the added \\(^ {12}\\text{CO}\\) gives A with **two \\(^ {12}\\text{CO}\\)** ligands |\n| **Path\u202f2** (\u00bd)        | \\(^ {12}\\text{CO}\\) inserts \u2192 acyl carbon = \\(^ {12}\\text{C}\\) | **\\(^ {12}\\)C** in acetyl iodide | The remaining original CO is \\(^ {13}\\text{CO}\\); together with the added \\(^ 